the full arm's fixture at runtime\". Look at run_trial in live.rs to see how fixture works — each trial gets its own fixture_root. Generating per-trial would mean wai init per trial — fine but the generation step in run_ablation happens once... The brief: \"`run_ablation` gains a generation step for the full arm\" — a single generation step, producing contents, then passed as extra_fixture_files. So there's a generation fixture dir — a temp dir where we invoke wai init once, capture outputs. That temp dir IS \"the fixture\" for generation purposes (the harness's fixture sandbox).\n\nSo design:\n\n```rust\npub fn generate_artifacts(fixture_dir: &Path, wai_bin: &Path) -> Result<GeneratedArtifacts, String>\n```\n\nWait, brief names it `AixArtifacts::for_fixture(fixture_dir, tool_binaries)` \"(name flexible)\". OK.\n\nThen how does run_ablation use it? Add opt-in via env or parameter. Simplest respecting both existing tests and \"run_ablation gains a generation step\":\n\nChange signature? That breaks all existing tests (they'd need updating — acceptable, tests/ablation.rs is in scope, and step 3 says \"without tool binaries / opt-out → hermetic fallback, behavior unchanged from today\").\n\nDesign: make `run_ablation` take `artifacts: &AixArtifacts` — keep — and add a new enum? Alternatively change `AixArtifacts` to hold owned `String`s and keep a constructor from &'static str for hermetic tests. Then run_ablation unchanged signature-wise, and generation step: new param or env.\n\nCleanest minimal-churn approach:\n\n1. Refactor `AixArtifacts` to own `String` fields; `AixArtifacts::hermetic(llms_txt, managed_agents_md, tools_toml)` accepting &'static str (tests unchanged mostly — test_artifacts() constructs struct literal with &str; update to use constructor or `.to_owned()`).\n\nHmm but brief says \"Static `&'static str` fields remain for hermetic tests\". So keep `AixArtifacts` exactly as-is with static fields, and add owned type + generation function + leak conversion? Leaking: `for_fixture` returns owned; production converts to AixArtifacts via leaking. Actually leaking strings in a one-shot CLI harness is fine and idiomatic enough, documented.\n\nBut then how does run_ablation invoke generation? run_ablation receives `&AixArtifacts` already generated. \"run_ablation gains a generation step for the full arm\" — maybe means: run_ablation accepts an `Option<&Path>` wai binary / or an `AixSource` enum:\n\n```rust\npub enum AixSource<'a> {\n    /// Hermetic static contents (default; CI without tool binaries).\n    Static(&'a AixArtifacts),\n    /// Real generation: invoke the provisioned `wai` binary in a\n    /// generation fixture dir; llms.txt via the genesis-vibes crate.\n    Real { wai: &'a Path },\n}\n```\n\nand run_ablation takes `artifacts: AixSource<'_>`. That changes all test call sites — but they pass `&test_artifacts()`; I'd update to `AixSource::Static(&test_artifacts())`. That's a big diff but explicit. Alternatively keep `&AixArtifacts` param and add `real_generation: Option<&Path>`. Hmm.\n\nEnv-based opt-in: brief says \"opt-in (env or parameter)\". Env opt-in keeps all existing signatures: `EVALLERINA_REAL_TOOLS=1` + locate wai binary. But hard to test step 1 (needs actual generation in test with assert on real content) — a test can construct its own temp dir, call `AixArtifacts::for_fixture` directly, and assert markers. And a run_ablation-level test with real generation: pass parameter. I'll go with parameter-based opt-in plus keep hermetic default.\n\nDecision: change run_ablation to take `artifacts: AixSource<'_>` where:\n\n```rust\npub enum AixSource<'a> {\n    Static(&'a AixArtifacts),\n    Real(ToolBinaries),\n}\n```\n\n`ToolBinaries { wai: PathBuf }`. In run_ablation:\n- collision check needs artifact paths — same three paths regardless of source, fine.\n- full arm artifact_files: Static → static contents; Real → generate via `AixArtifacts::for_fixture(gen_dir, &binaries)` in a temp dir (tempfile? not in deps — genesis-vibes depends on tempfile but we can't use its dep directly. Add tempfile to evallerina deps? Or use std::env::temp_dir + unique name). Check if evallerina already uses tempfile in dev-deps.\n\nActually, could avoid a temp dir: invoke wai init in the generation dir = a temp dir we create. Let me check Cargo.toml dev-deps and whether tempfile is used anywhere."}}
