ixture_files(),\\n         AixSource::Real(tools) => generate_artifacts(tools)?,  // returns owned Vec\\n     };\\n     ```\\n   - `generate_artifacts` creates a generation fixture dir under temp_dir (unique), runs wai init, reads outputs, computes llms.txt via crate, cleans up.\\n\\nBut run_ablation is invoked per ablation; generating per call is fine. Where's the \\\"generation step for the full arm\\\"? Inside run_ablation when Real. \\n\\nHmm wait — but for the full arm, the artifacts are written into each trial's fixture by live.rs. The generation fixture is separate (a temp dir). Brief: \\\"let the provisioned tools generate their artifacts inside the full arm's fixture at runtime\\\" — generating in a temp fixture then provisioning contents achieves the same, and per-trial wai init would be wasteful. I'll generate once in a dedicated generation fixture dir.\\n\\n6. Step 4 red test: \\\"tool generation failure → loud error (corrupt-config scenario owns .genesis/tools.toml — do not collide; see artifact_path_collision test)\\\". So: real generation with a wai binary that fails (exit nonzero) → run_ablation returns Err naming the failure. Test with a fake \\\"wai\\\" binary (shell script that exits 1) via ToolBinaries{wai: path_to_failing_script}. Assert run_ablation errs. Also the collision note: in Real mode, `.genesis/tools.toml` collision with corrupt-config scenario → the collision check fires before generation, so no generation happens — add test that collision is still loud in Real mode? The step-4 parenthetical suggests ensuring real generation doesn't collide with scenario-owned .genesis/tools.toml. Since our generation writes to a separate temp dir, there's no collision risk with the scenario fixture at all. The existing artifact_path_collision test covers Static; add a Real-mode collision test.\\n\\n7. Step 1 red test (real AGENTS.md): \\n   ```rust\\n   #[test]\\n   fn real_generation_writes_managed_agents_md_with_wai_provenance()\\n   ```\\n   - needs real wai binary. Hermetic gating: if wai not found on PATH → skip (return early). Locally wai exists, so tier0 runs it for real. Use a Sandbox-like temp dir, call `AixArtifacts::for_fixture` or run run_ablation with AixSource::Real and the discovered wai, assert the fixture AGENTS.md contains \\\"<!-- WAI:START -->\\\" and \\\"<!-- WAI:END -->\\\" and \\\"provenance: generator=wai\\\" — not the placeholder text.\\n\\n   But the artifacts_scenario's check requires llms.txt in fixture; with real generation llms.txt is written too, so full arm passes. Assert on fixture AGENTS.md content — but trials run and fixtures... do trial fixture dirs persist? Each trial creates fixture_root; check how run_trial creates it — scenario fixture files written per trial into its own dir. The test can't easily access per-trial fixture root... unless checks read it. Better: test `for_fixture` directly (unit-level contract of the generation step) plus a run_ablation-level test asserting full arm passes with real artifacts (check reads llms.txt) — and assert generated content via for_fixture's return value.\\n\\n   For step 1 test shape: call `AixArtifacts::for_fixture(&gen_dir, &ToolBinaries{wai})` → assert `managed_agents_md` contains WAI:START marker, WAI:END marker, \\\"provenance: generator=wai\\\", and NOT the placeholder text (\\\"<!-- WAI:START -->managed guidance\\\").\\n\\n   Genesis managed_block markers from the crate: use `genesis::managed_block::BlockDef::new(\\\"WAI\\\")` to derive markers rather than hardcoding — brief says \\\"assert contains the block markers from genesis-vibes managed_block\\\". \\n\\n8. Step 2 red test: llms.txt is genesis generate_llms_txt output: compute expected via the crate with same inputs? That would duplicate internals. \\\"contains module section markers\\\" — assert generated `llms_txt` contains \\\"## Modules\\\" and the tool names registered (e.g. \\\"wai\\\"), plus `# {project}` title? Assert \\\"## Modules\\\" and \\\"- `wai`\\\" — hmm, generate_llms_txt formats modules as \\\"- `name` — description\\\". Assert contains \\\"## Modules\\\" and \\\"`wai`\\\". Also assert it equals `generate_llms_txt` called with the ProjectMeta/ModuleEntry inputs we passed — that's fine, it's the same crate call, verifying plumbing: expected = generate_llms_txt(&meta, &modules); assert_eq!(artifacts.llms_txt, expected). That directly pins \\\"llms.txt content is genesis generate_llms_txt output\\\". Good — no duplication issue since for_fixture takes the modules as inputs (from manifest). But test needs to know meta/modules used. Make for_fixture derive ProjectMeta from... hmm. Design for_fixture signature to accept the meta? `for_fixture(fixture_dir, tool_binaries)` per brief — meta derived internally. For the equality test, recompute expected using the same derivation — brittle if derivation is internal. Alternative: for_fixture returns GeneratedAixArtifacts; test asserts structural markers: starts with \\\"# \\\", contains \\\"## Modules\\\", contains \\\"## Quick start\\\"? meta.name/tagline derived from fixture dir name → \\\"# tmp-xyz\\\". Assert llms_txt.contains(\\\"## Modules\\\") and contains(\\\"`wai`\\\") and contains tagline? Simpler: assert_eq against a re-derived call in the test with the same public inputs. I can expose the derivation as a public helper `llms_txt_for(manifest_tools) -> (ProjectMeta, modules)`? Getting heavy. \\n\\n   Keep it pragmatic: for_fixture takes `ToolBinaries` AND the fixture_dir; ProjectMeta built from `fixture_dir.file_name()` as name + tagline \\\"the dulce-de-leche tool family\\\". Test: create sandbox named \\\"dulce-fixture\\\", run for_fixture, expected = genesis::aix::generate_llms_txt(&ProjectMeta{name:\\\"dulce-fixture\\\", tagline: \\\"...\\\", None,None,None}, &modules_from(manifest)). The manifest is created by wai init — deterministic content (\\\"Workflow manager for AI-assisted development\\\"). So test can build modules from the known registration. Meh — duplication. \\n\\n   Better: assert on markers: `llms_txt` starts with `# dulce-fixture\\\\n` (crate format: `# {name}\\\\n\\\\n`), contains \\\"## Modules\\\", contains \\\"`wai`\\\", and contains the description from tools.toml (\\\"Workflow manager for AI-assisted development\\\"). That asserts it's crate-shaped output for the fixture tool set without duplicating the exact bytes. Plus assert it's NOT the placeholder (\\\"llms.txt content: wai status --json\\\"). Good enough for the contract.\\n\\n9. Also update tests' existing call sites run_ablation(&test_artifacts()) → AixSource::Static.\\n\\n10. Header comment update in ablation.rs.\\n\\nNote `AixArtifacts` is `Copy` with static fields — keep. `GeneratedAixArtifacts` owned. run_ablation consumes `AixSource` by value (enum holds refs/borrows).\\n\\nCollision check in Real mode: paths are fixed (\\\"AGENTS.md\\\", \\\".genesis/tools.toml\\\", \\\"llms.txt\\\") — check those against scenario material before generating.\\n\\nGeneration failure modes (step 4):\\n- wai binary spawn failure (not found) → loud Err\\n- nonzero exit → Err with stderr snippet\\n- missing produced files (no AGENTS.md) → Err\\n- no tools.toml → Err? wai init produces it; missing = failure (loud). Yes.\\n\\nAlso \\\"no silent fallback on tool failure\\\": run_ablation with Real that fails returns Err (ablation aborts). Test: failing fake wai script → run_ablation Err contains \\\"wai init\\\".\\n\\nFake wai binary for failure test: create a shell script `#!/bin/sh\\\\nexit 3` chmod +x. Also success case uses real wai from PATH (skip if absent).\\n\\nEnv opt-in: I'll also wire `AixSource::real_opt_in() -> Self` reading EVALLERINA_REAL_TOOLS + PATH discovery, defaulting Static. Hmm, adds surface not demanded by red/green steps. The brief says \\\"(env or parameter)\\\" — parameter suffices. But then step 3's \\\"without tool binaries / opt-out\\\" test: how do we test \\\"behavior unchanged\\\" without a path that would attempt real generation? With the enum there IS no auto-fallback — Static is explicit. The red test for step 3: run_ablation with AixSource::Static provisions the static contents (assert via a check that AGENTS.md equals the static placeholder... checks only get fixture_root). Simpler: assert the full arm passes and, better, use a scenario whose check reads AGENTS.md content equality with the static text. Scenario checks receive r.fixture_root — can read AGENTS.md. \\n\\nWait, but with the enum design, is \\\"behavior unchanged from today\\\" literally satisfied for existing callers? Existing callers pass &AixArtifacts; they must wrap in AixSource::Static — signature change is part of this ticket's green. Fine.\\n\\nAlternatively — reconsider a lighter-touch design honoring \\\"fallback\\\": keep run_ablation signature `artifacts: &AixArtifacts` unchanged, add NEW function `run_ablation_real(scenario, models, reps, wai: &Path, factories, bounds, budget)` that generates contents (leak into AixArtifacts) and delegates. Then tests unchanged except new ones. Less churn, honors \\\"production entry returns owned contents\\\" less well... The brief explicitly describes extending, name flexible. I prefer the enum: single entry, explicit arms, no leak. But leak-based `AixArtifacts::from_owned` keeps existing call sites untouched... I'll go with the enum; updating tests is trivial and honest.\\n\\nHmm, one more consideration: `run_ablation(artifacts: AixSource<'_>)` — by value; AixSource::Static(&'a AixArtifacts) copies the ref. Fine.\\n\\nAlso live.rs plumbing: ArmContext unchanged — extra_fixture_files already generic over contents. No live.rs changes needed. Good (brief says \\\"only if required\\\").\\n\\nNow the header of src/ablation.rs must be updated (file-headers convention: Purpose/Responsibilities/Rationale).\\n\\nLet me now write the code.\\n\\nRed/green discipline: The brief wants committing per green step: step1 commit, step2, step3, step4. To do red→green properly I'd write test first, watch fail, implement, commit. I'll follow: implement step 1 test + minimal generation (AGENTS.md via wai init) → test green → commit. Then step 2 (llms.txt) — but for_fixture returns all artifacts... To honor per-step commits, implement incrementally: step 1: for_fixture generating managed AGENTS.md (+ tools.toml since wai init produces it anyway) but llms.txt still placeholder? Hmm — \\\"no placeholder content in the real path\\\" is the anti-goal, but intermediate commits are fine as long as final state is clean. Step 1 green: generate AGENTS.md real, llms.txt/tools.toml... tools.toml comes free from wai init. llms.txt: keep placeholder in step 1 commit? That leaves real path partially placeholder in an intermediate commit — acceptable process artifact, final commit clean. Actually simpler: step 1 implements for_fixture for AGENTS.md + tools.toml (from wai init outputs), llms.txt empty→ hmm empty is bad.\\n\\nPlan the diff across commits:\\n- Commit 1 (step 1): AixSource enum + ToolBinaries + for_fixture returning AGENTS.md (real) & tools.toml (real from wai init) & llms.txt = placeholder static for now (marked TODO step 2). Tests: real-generation AGENTS.md test + collision in Real mode + failure loud error? No — failure test is step 4. Keep step 1 test set: real AGENTS.md test.\\n- Commit 2 (step 2): llms.txt via genesis::aix::generate_llms_txt from manifest modules; test asserts markers/not-placeholder.\\n- Commit 3 (step 3): hermetic default unchanged: test that AixSource::Static provisions static contents (and maybe that run_ablation with Static behaves byte-identically — existing tests already pin that; add an explicit test asserting the full arm's AGENTS.md equals the static placeholder text via a content check).\\n- Commit 4 (step 4): loud error on generation failure: fake failing wai script → Err; also binary-not-found → Err.\\n\\nThat works.\\n\\nNow details of implementation.\\n\\n```rust\\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\\n\\n/// Provisioned tool binaries available for real artifact generation\\n/// ([`AixSource::Real`]). Paths, not versions: generation asserts on\\n/// process exit codes and produced files, never tool internals.\\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\\npub struct ToolBinaries {\\n    /// The provisioned `wai` binary (generates the managed AGENTS.md\\n    /// and registers the tool manifest).\\n    pub wai: PathBuf,\\n}\\n\\nimpl ToolBinaries {\\n    /// Locate `wai` on `PATH`; `None` when absent (CI without tool\\n    /// binaries stays hermetic).\\n    pub fn discover_wai() -> Option<PathBuf> { ... }\\n}\\n```\\n\\ndiscover: iterate PATH split, check file exists and is executable. On unix fine.\\n\\n```rust\\n/// Which contents the full arm provisions.\\npub enum AixSource<'a> {\\n    /// Hermetic static contents (tests; CI without tool binaries).\\n    Static(&'a AixArtifacts),\\n    /// Real generation: run the provisioned tools in a generation\\n    /// fixture and harvest what they produce. Opt-in.\\n    Real(ToolBinaries),\\n}\\n```\\n\\n```rust\\n/// Owned artifact contents produced by real generation\\n/// ([`AixArtifacts::for_fixture`]).\\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\\npub struct GeneratedAixArtifacts {\\n    pub llms_txt: String,\\n    pub managed_agents_md: String,\\n    pub tools_toml: String,\\n}\\n\\nimpl GeneratedAixArtifacts {\\n    pub fn fixture_files(&self) -> Vec<(String, String)> { same mapping }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHmm wait — brief names `AixArtifacts::for_fixture(fixture_dir, tool_binaries)`. So make it an associated fn on AixArtifacts returning GeneratedAixArtifacts. OK.\\n\\n```rust\\nimpl AixArtifacts {\\n    /// Real generation entry: invoke the provisioned tools inside\\n    /// `fixture_dir` and harvest the artifacts they produce.\\n    /// Process-boundary only: assert `wai init --json` exit code 0 and\\n    /// the produced files; never tool internals. Fails loudly (Err) —\\n    /// never a silent placeholder fallback.\\n    pub fn for_fixture(\\n        fixture_dir: &Path,\\n        tools: &ToolBinaries,\\n    ) -> Result<GeneratedAixArtifacts, String> {\\n        // wai init --json inside the fixture: real managed AGENTS.md\\n        // (provenance footer + managed block) and the real registered\\n        // `.genesis/tools.toml`.\\n        let out = std::process::Command::new(&tools.wai)\\n            .arg(\\\"init\\\")\\n            .arg(\\\"--json\\\")\\n            .current_dir(fixture_dir)\\n            .output()\\n            .map_err(|e| format!(\\\"wai init spawn failed ({}): {e}\\\", tools.wai.display()))?;\\n        if !out.status.success() {\\n            return Err(format!(\\n                \\\"wai init exited {} — real artifact generation failed loudly (stderr: {})\\\",\\n                out.status,\\n                String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stderr).trim()\\n            ));\\n        }\\n        let agents_path = fixture_dir.join(\\\"AGENTS.md\\\");\\n        let managed_agents_md = std::fs::read_to_string(&agents_path)\\n            .map_err(|e| format!(\\\"wai init produced no readable AGENTS.md: {e}\\\"))?;\\n        let tools_path = fixture_dir.join(\\\".genesis/tools.toml\\\");\\n        let tools_toml = std::fs::read_to_string(&tools_path)\\n            .map_err(|e| format!(\\\"wai init produced no readable .genesis/tools.toml: {e}\\\"))?;\\n        let llms_txt = Self::llms_txt_for_fixture(fixture_dir, &tools_toml)?;\\n        Ok(GeneratedAixArtifacts { llms_txt, managed_agents_md, tools_toml })\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWait — validate managed block markers in for_fixture? The produced AGENTS.md must contain the WAI block; step-1 test asserts markers. Should for_fixture itself assert? \\\"assert on exit codes + produced files, never internals\\\" — markers are part of the produced file contract. I'll assert contains start marker in for_fixture too (loud error otherwise). Hmm, but the test wants to assert contains markers; implementation asserting the same is fine (test still meaningful via direct content assertions + provenance footer).\\n\\nActually keep for_fixture minimal: exit code + produced files readable + non-empty. Markers asserted in the test (step 1) and provenance footer. If for_fixture over-asserts, a legit variant would fail. But \\\"assert on exit codes + produced files\\\" — I'll assert produced files exist and are non-empty. The test asserts the block markers.\\n\\nllms_txt generation (step 2):\\n\\n```rust\\nfn llms_txt_for_fixture(fixture_dir: &Path, tools_toml: &str) -> Result<String, String> {\\n    let manifest: genesis::discovery::Manifest = toml::from_str(tools_toml)\\n        .map_err(|e| format!(\\\"generated .genesis/tools.toml is not valid TOML: {e}\\\"))?;\\n    let mut modules: Vec<genesis::aix::ModuleEntry> = manifest.tools.into_iter()\\n        .map(|(name, entry)| genesis::aix::ModuleEntry { name, description: entry.description })\\n        .collect();\\n    modules.sort_by(|a, b| a.name.cmp(&b.name));\\n    let name = fixture_dir.file_name().and_then(|n| n.to_str()).unwrap_or(\\\"dulce-fixture\\\").to_owned();\\n    let meta = genesis::aix::ProjectMeta {\\n        name,\\n        tagline: \\\"the dulce-de-leche tool family\\\".to_owned(),\\n        repository: None, documentation: None, crates_io: None,\\n    };\\n    Ok(genesis::aix::generate_llms_txt(&meta, &modules))\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHmm \\\"description\\\" default empty when absent — ToolEntry.description has serde default; fine.\\n\\nDeterministic order: HashMap iteration random — sort for stable llms.txt.\\n\\nrun_ablation:\\n\\n```rust\\npub fn run_ablation(\\n    scenario: &Scenario,\\n    models: &[String],\\n    reps: u32,\\n    artifacts: AixSource<'_>,\\n    factories: AblationFactories<'_>,\\n    bounds: &Bounds,\\n    budget_remaining: &dyn Fn() -> bool,\\n) -> Result<AblationReport, String> {\\n    // floor check\\n    // collision check (paths fixed for both sources)\\n    let artifact_files: Vec<(String, String)> = match &artifacts {\\n        AixSource::Static(a) => a.fixture_files(),\\n        AixSource::Real(tools) => {\\n            let gen_dir = generation_fixture();\\n            let generated = AixArtifacts::for_fixture(&gen_dir, tools)?;\\n            let files = generated.fixture_files();\\n            // cleanup: remove gen_dir? best-effort.\\n            files\\n        }\\n    };\\n    ...\\n}\\n```\\n\\ncollision check must happen with the fixed paths regardless of source:\\n\\n```rust\\nconst ARTIFACT_PATHS: [&str; 3] = [\\\"AGENTS.md\\\", \\\".genesis/tools.toml\\\", \\\"llms.txt\\\"];\\nfor path in ARTIFACT_PATHS { ... }\\n```\\n\\nBut careful: static fixture_files() currently returns those exact paths; keep a shared const so they can't drift. Existing test `artifact_fixture_files_map_the_three_paths` asserts AixArtifacts::fixture_files paths — refactor fixture_files to use ARTIFACT_PATHS order.\\n\\ngeneration_fixture(): temp dir unique per call:\\n```rust\\nfn generation_fixture() -> Result<PathBuf, String> {\\n    let dir = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!(\\n        \\\"evallerina-ablation-gen-{}-{}\\\",\\n        std::process::id(),\\n        std::time::SystemTime::now().duration_since(std::time::UNIX_UNIX_EPOCH)...nanos\\n    ));\\n    std::fs::create_dir_all(&dir).map_err(...)?;\\n    Ok(dir)\\n}\\n```\\nCleanup best-effort with remove_dir_all after harvesting (ignore errors).\\n\\nNow step 3 test (hermetic unchanged): a scenario check that reads AGENTS.md and compares to the static placeholder:\\n\\n```rust\\n#[test]\\nfn static_source_is_unchanged_hermetic_behavior() {\\n    let scenario = Scenario::new(\\\"static-probe\\\", \\\"task\\\").check(\\\"agents-md-is-static\\\", |r| {\\n        let md = std::fs::read_to_string(r.fixture_root.join(\\\"AGENTS.md\\\")).unwrap_or_default();\\n        if md == \\\"<!-- WAI:START -->managed guidance<!-- WAI:END -->\\\" { pass } else { fail }\\n    });\\n    run_ablation(..., AixSource::Static(&test_artifacts()), ...) → full passed == 3\\n}\\n```\\n\\nBut does the trial fixture get torn down after the check? Check runs against fixture root while it exists — scenario.rs checks receive `r` with fixture_root; if fixture removed after check, check still reads during run. Fine.\\n\\nAlso step 3's \\\"without tool binaries\\\": ToolBinaries::discover_wai() → None → caller can't build Real → hermetic. Test: if discover returns None... skip. Add a test `real_generation_skips_when_wai_absent`? That tests nothing when wai present. Instead: test that `AixSource` default resolution helper... skip; the Static test covers hermetic behavior. I'll document.\\n\\nStep 4 tests:\\n\\n```rust\\n#[test]\\nfn generation_failure_is_a_loud_error() {\\n    // fake wai that exits 3\\n    let script = sandbox/\\\"fail-wai\\\"; write \\\"#!/bin/sh\\\\necho boom >&2\\\\nexit 3\\\\n\\\"; chmod +x (set perms via fs metadata on unix)\\n    let err = AixArtifacts::for_fixture(&gen_dir, &ToolBinaries{wai: script}).expect_err(\\\"loud\\\");\\n    assert!(err.contains(\\\"wai init\\\"));\\n    // and run_ablation with Real propagates the error (ablation aborts, no silent placeholder)\\n    let err = run_ablation(..., AixSource::Real(ToolBinaries{wai: script}), ...).expect_err(...);\\n    assert!(err.contains(\\\"wai init\\\"));\\n}\\n\\n#[test]\\nfn missing_produced_file_is_a_loud_error() {\\n    // fake wai that exits 0 but writes nothing\\n    \\\"#!/bin/sh\\\\nexit 0\\\\n\\\" → Err mentions AGENTS.md\\n}\\n```\\n\\nchmod on unix: `std::os::unix::fs::PermissionsExt::from_mode(0o755)`.\\n\\nNote: FakeTransport-based run_ablation tests don't touch a real fixture root for the real artifacts? run_trial writes fixture files per trial — Real mode in failing test: generation fails BEFORE any trial runs → Err before factories used. Factory closure must still be passed; fine (unused). But `green_factory()` returns impl FnMut — passing `&mut green_factory()` fine.\\n\\nWait: in the failing run_ablation Real test, the collision check happens first (scenario = artifacts_scenario, no collisions), then generation fails → Err. \\n\\nReal-mode success run_ablation test (step 1/2 integration): with real wai (skip if absent):\\n\\n```rust\\n#[test]\\nfn real_generation_full_arm_provisions_real_contents() {\\n    let Some(wai) = ToolBinaries::discover_wai() else { return; }; // hermetic skip in CI\\n    let report = run_ablation(&artifacts_scenario(), ..., AixSource::Real(ToolBinaries{wai}), ...);\\n    assert full passed == 3 (llms.txt present in fixture)\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHmm — skip-on-absent: \\\"no silent fallback\\\" — but this is a test-gating choice, and step 3 says CI without binaries runs hermetically. Acceptable and documented.\\n\\nAlso step 1 test needs a generation dir: for_fixture requires caller-provided fixture_dir. In the for_fixture unit test, create a fresh temp dir. Note wai init in a dir whose name has weird chars fine.\\n\\nOne catch: wai init in a temp dir — wai writes .wai/, .genesis/, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md. Fine. But does wai init require git? It ran fine in /tmp. Good.\\n\\nAnother: tests run in parallel; each creates unique temp dirs.\\n\\nEdge: `wai init` might fail if run twice in same dir (already_initialized). Each test uses fresh dir. OK.\\n\\nAlso `AixArtifacts` is Copy — `AixSource::Static(&AixArtifacts)`; test_artifacts() returns AixArtifacts by value; `AixSource::Static(&test_artifacts())` — temporary lifetime issue: `run_ablation(..., AixSource::Static(&test_artifacts()), ...)` — temporary in function call arg lives for the call. Fine.\\n\\nNow clippy -D warnings: watch for unused imports etc.\\n\\nAlso update file header of src/ablation.rs (Purpose/Responsibilities/Rationale) to mention real generation.\\n\\nlib.rs: check what's re-exported from ablation.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-6abc4b2f-ed13-45e5-8da3-8729588eeeeb","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cat src/lib.rs","timeout":10}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":9357,"output":7005,"cacheRead":22400,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6985,"totalTokens":38762,"cost":{"input":0.00140355,"output":0.0035025,"cacheRead":0.000672,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00557805}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792433142902,"responseId":"gen-1792433142-YLT0CG0X6cec7zrEQJwJ","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
